# Nanoscale Insights into Photovoltaic Hysteresis in Triple‐Cation Mixed‐Halide Perovskite: Resolving the Role of Polarization and Ionic Migration

## abstract
Triple-cation mixed-halide perovskites of composition Csx (FAy MA1- y )1 -x Pb(Iz Br1 -z )3 (CsFAMA) have been reported to possess excellent photovoltaic efficiency with minimal hysteresis; in this work, nanoscale insight is shed into the roles of illumination-induced polarization and ionic migration in photovoltaic hysteresis. By examining the concurrent evolution of ionic distribution and spontaneous polarization of CsFAMA under light illumination using dynamic-strain-based scanning probe microscopy, strong linear piezoelectricity arising from photoenhanced polarization is observed, while ionic migration is found to be not significantly increased by lightening. Nanoscale photocurrents are mapped under a series of biases using conductive atomic force microscopy, revealing negligible difference between forward and backward scans, and local IV curves reconstructed from principal component analysis show minimal hysteresis of just 1%. These observations at the nanoscale are confirmed in a macroscopic perovskite solar cell made of CsFAMA, exhibiting a high efficiency of 20.11% and with hysteresis index as small as 3%. Ionic migration, polarization, and photocurrent hysteresis are thus directly correlated at the nanoscale, and photoenhanced polarization in triple-cation mixed-halide perovskites is established, which does not contribute to the photovoltaic hysteresis.

Triple-cation mixed-halide perovskites of composition $\mathsf{C s}_{x}(\mathsf{F A}_{\gamma}\mathsf{M A}_{1-\gamma})_{1-x}\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{I}_{z}\mathsf{B r}_{1-z})_{3}$ (CsFAMA) have been reported to possess excellent photovoltaic efficiency with minimal hysteresis; in this work, nanoscale insight is shed into the roles of illumination-induced polarization and ionic migration in photovoltaic hysteresis. By examining the concurrent evolution of ionic distribution and spontaneous polarization of CsFAMA under light illumination using dynamic-strain-based scanning probe microscopy, strong linear piezoelectricity arising from photo­ enhanced polarization is observed, while ionic migration is found to be not significantly increased by lightening. Nanoscale photocurrents are mapped under a series of biases using conductive atomic force microscopy, revealing negligible difference between forward and backward scans, and local IV curves reconstructed from principal component analysis show minimal hysteresis of just $1\%$ . These observations at the nanoscale are confirmed in a macroscopic perovskite solar cell made of CsFAMA, exhibiting a high efficiency of $20.11\%$ and with hysteresis index as small as $3\%$ . Ionic migration, polarization, and photocurrent hysteresis are thus directly correlated at the nanoscale, and photoenhanced polarization in triple-cation mixed-halide perovskites is established, which does not contribute to the photovoltaic hysteresis.
Halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have received much attention in the last ten years thanks to their superior photo­ voltaic properties and rapidly rising power conversion efficiency (PCE).[1–6] While the original development started with $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ ,[1] much of recent effort has been devoted to multiple-cation mixed-halide perovskites, which have been found to be relatively stable with suppressed ionic migration, reduced photovoltaic hyster­ esis,[7–10] and record efficiency, for example in triple-cation mixed-halide perovskite of composition $\mathrm{Cs}_{x}(\mathrm{FA}_{\gamma}\mathrm{MA}_{1-\gamma})_{1-x}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{z}\mathrm{Br}_{1-z})_{3}$ (CsFAMA).[8–10] The photovoltaic hyster­ esis of PSCs is particularly interesting and intriguing.[11] While initially thought to be related to possible ferroelectric switching of organic-inorganic hybrid perovskites,[12,13] the prevailing view in the field now is that it is more closely related to ionic migration.[14–16] For example, by demonstrating strong frequency dependence in conductance hysteresis of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , it has been concluded by Beilsten-Edmands et al. that the observed hyster­ etic conductance can be explained by the migration of mobile ions.[17] Furthermore, it was estimated that there is an upper limit of $1\upmu\mathrm{C}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ on intrinsic frequency-independent polariza­ tion, if any, ruling out ferroelectricity as the main cause of cur­ rent–voltage hysteresis for that particular system.[17] A series of other studies have provided further evidences on the relation­ ship between ionic migration and photovoltaic hysteresis.[18–20]
The role of ferroelectricity in photovoltaic hysteresis, however, is more tricky, as the very existence of ferroelectric polarization in halide perovskites is still hotly debated.[21–24] Nevertheless, there are strong evidences suggesting that the energetic differ­ ence between ferroelectric polar and dielectric nonpolar phases of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ is rather small, and thus depending on composi­ tions, substrates, and processing conditions, the halide perov­ skites could be either ferroelectric or nonferroelectric, or mixed with alternating polar and nonpolar domains.[25] Importantly, there is also an indication that the ferroelectric polar order and the ionic migration could be intimately coupled in $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ .[26] While in the absence of strong ferroelectricity, it has been con­ cluded that the dominant mechanism responsible for photovol­ taic hysteresis is ionic migration, the role of polarization in a ferroelectric halide perovskite remains to be examined. This is particularly important for triple-cation mixed-halide perovskites, as their possible polar nature has not been reported, to our best knowledge.
To resolve the relative contributions of polarization and ionic migration in photovoltaic hysteresis, it is important to compare ionic migration and spontaneous polarization of halide perovskites, especially their concurrent evolution under light illumination. The macroscopic measurement, how­ ever, is virtually impossible, since the sample is quite leaky that smears any possible polarization effect.[12,27] We thus have to examine local polarization and ionic migration at the nanoscale. Dynamic-strain-based scanning probe microscopy (ds-SPM) provides a powerful tool to map the local distribu­ tions of both polarization and mobile ions with nanoscale resolution,[28] making it possible to assess their relative con­ tribution to photovoltaic hysteresis in combination with local photocurrent measurement. We thus use these techniques to investigate triple-cation mixed-halide perovskite CsFAMA and demonstrate its strongly enhanced spontaneous polarization under light illumination, while the ionic migration is found to increase only slightly. This leads to minimal photovoltaic hysteresis measured at the nanoscale via photoconductive AFM (pc-AFM) in combination with principal component analysis (PCA),[29–31] with which we construct local $I V$ curves taking into account leaking resistance.[32,33] As a result, small hysteresis index of only $3\%$ was measured at macroscopic scale in the PSC device, which is fully consistent with the nanoscale characterizations. This is the first work that correlates ionic migration, polarization, and photocurrent hysteresis at the nanoscale, and establishes the photoenhanced polarization in triple-cation mixed-halide perovskite that does not contribute to the photovoltaic hysteresis.
Triple-cation mixed-halide perovskite of composition $\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.81}M A_{0.14}P b I_{2.55}B r_{0.45}}$ (CsFAMA) was deposited on glass/fluorine-doped tin oxide $\mathrm{(FTO)}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ substrate,[34] chosen in this study for its stability and small hysteresis.[9] An AC voltage was applied via a conductive atomic force microscope (AFM) probe to excite the electromechanical vibration of the sample, as schematically shown in Figure S1a in the Supporting Information, and the dynamic strain response was measured via AFM photodiode, yielding not only response amplitude, but also contact resonant frequency and quality factor (Figure S1b, Supporting Information).[28,35,36] The technique is known as pie­ zoresponse force microscopy (PFM) when the sample is ferro­ electric with spontaneous polarization and piezoelectricity,[36–38] and as electrochemical strain microscopy (ESM) when the dominant response arises from ionic Vegard strain and elec­ trochemical dipoles.[39–42] As such, it is capable of probing both spontaneous polarization and ionic distribution in the sample, and we refer to the measured signals as piezoresponse regard­ less of their microscopic mechanisms.
We are interested in the evolution of polarization and ionic migration induced by lightening, and thus we first examine the local piezoresponse in the dark (Figure 1a–d) and under a vis­ ible light LED illumination (Figure  1e–h) subjected to an AC voltage of $2{\mathrm{~V}},$ wherein mappings of topography, piezoresponse amplitude, contact resonant frequency, and quality factor are presented, derived from dual-amplitude resonance tracking (DART) technique in combination with damped simple har­ monic oscillator model (DSHO).[36] It is immediately evident by comparing the mappings in Figure  1b,f that there is sub­ stantially enhanced piezoresponse under light illumination, which is also made clear by their respective histogram distribu­ tions (Figure  1j). The piezoresponse amplitude in the dark is measured to be $8.2\pm4.0~\mathrm{pm}$ , while under light illumination it increases by more than $100\%$ to $17.4\pm5.3$ pm. Interestingly, such substantial piezoresponse enhancement arises without notable change in topography (Figure 1a,e,i) or in quality factor (Figure $^\mathrm{1d,h,l}$ ), while the contact resonant frequency decreases slightly from $325.8\pm5.1$ to $323.6\pm5.7\mathrm{~kHz}$ under light illu­ mination (Figure  1c,g,k). This is in contrast to a recent study where preexisting periodically striped ferroelastic domains are observed to be modulated by light or electric field, resulting in significant topography variation.[43]
It is now well known that both spontaneous polarization and ionic concentration oscillation under an AC voltage contribute to piezoresponse measured by ds-SPM,[44] and thus the question is what is responsible for the substantially enhanced piezore­ sponse under light illumination. The ionic migration is an energy dissipative process,[45] and if it is enhanced under light illumination, then the quality factor, whose inverse correlates with energy dissipation,[46] should decrease. This is not what we observe in Figure 1d,h,l. Quite contrarily, the quality factor does not change much here, suggesting that the enhanced piezore­ sponse may primarily arise from the enhanced polarization and piezoelectric effect, which is reversible and thus energy conserv­ ative.[47] This hypothesis is also consistent with the reduced con­ tact resonant frequency under light illumination (Figure 1c,g,k), since enhanced piezoelectricity is often accompanied by sof­ tening in elasticity.[48,49] To confirm this hypothesis, we thus compare the evolution of the point-wise first and second har­ monic piezoresponse in the dark and under light illumination as schematically shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information). Note that the first harmonic piezoresponse mainly arises from the intrinsic piezoelectricity due to the presence of spontaneous polarization, hereafter referred to as polarization strain, while the second harmonic one is induced primarily by the electro­ chemical dipoles arising from ionic redistribution,[50] hereafter referred to as ionic strain. Comparing first harmonic polari­ zation strain and second harmonic ionic strain thus gives us a clear indication whether polarization or ionic migration is enhanced by light illumination.
We have probed five spatial points for the first and second harmonic piezoresponse measurements in the dark and under illumination, with representative curves for polarization and ionic strains versus AC frequencies in Figure  2a,e at one par­ ticular point under an AC excitation of $1.8~\mathrm{V}_{:}$ , chosen for its modest excitation voltage and good signal-to-noise ratio. Addi­ tional data are shown in Figure S3 (Supporting Information) under a series of different excitation voltages up to $3\ \mathrm{V}.$ . While the nominal voltages applied to the probe exceed the open-cir­ cuit voltage of PSCs, there is a significant voltage drop expected between the probe and the sample, and the actual voltage on the sample is smaller. As such, we anticipate these high-frequency
AC voltages probe but not interfere with the ionic distribution in the sample. Note that the first harmonic polarization strain is substantially enhanced by light illumination (Figure  2a), while the enhancement is rather minor for second harmonic ionic strain (Figure 2e), supporting the idea that it is primarily spon­ taneous polarization, not ionic migration, that is enhanced by light illumination here. Furthermore, these curves are fitted well by DSHO model,[46,51] yielding the corrected polarization and ionic strains averaged over five points versus AC voltage in the dark and under illumination (Figure  2b,f), as well as the corresponding quality factor (Figure  2c,g) and contact res­ onant frequency (Figure  2d,h). Note that the contact resonant frequencies determined from the first and second harmonic piezoresponses are consistent with each other (Figures  2d,h), demonstrating the high fidelity of the measurement. Impor­ tantly, the trend observed in a single point (Figure 2a,e) is found to be valid after statistic averaging, where the linear polariza­ tion strain is substantially enhanced by the light illumination (Figure  2b), while the quadratic ionic strain only increases modestly (Figure  2f). The quality factor for the first harmonic response actually increases significantly due to higher signalto-noise ratio (Figure  2b), while the second harmonic quality factor sees much smaller increase. For both the first and second harmonic measurements, we observe a consistent reduction in contact resonant frequency under light illumination, or the sof­ tening of the elasticity. All these data suggest that it is primarily spontaneous polarization that is enhanced by light illumination in CsFAMA, while ionic migration is not affected much. To this end, we point out that while ds-SPM probes the sample via a high-frequency excitation around $300~\mathrm{kHz}$ , which is too fast for long-range ionic migration, we actually compare the response in the dark and under light illumination, so that the time scale involved is much longer, around 3 min of light illumination, enabling us to decipher possible difference in local ionic dis­ tribution induced by lightening.[52] We also note minor quanti­ tative inconsistency between spatial mappings of Figure 1 and point-wise statistics of Figure 2, particularly in the quality factor. While both are derived from DSHO model, spatial mappings in Figure 1 rely on DART measurements at only two excitation frequencies to solve the highly nonlinear equations,[36] which is approximate in nature and not expected to be very accurate quantitatively, while point-wise studies in Figure 2 use the com­ plete spectrum data to fit the DSHO model,[53] which is highly reliable.
What is the photovoltaic implication that light illumination substantially enhances polarization but not ionic migration, especially on its hysteresis? To this end, we map the local photo­ current of the sample by pc-AFM[30] under a series of forward and backward biases applied to the sample ranging from 0 to $0.7{\mathrm{~V~}}$ at a step of $0.1~\mathrm{V},$ with selected mappings shown in Figure  3 along with comparison of histogram distribution under the same forward and backward biases. Additional data are shown in Figure S4 (Supporting Information). First of all, we note strong short-circuit current without bias (Figure 3a,e,i), calculated to be $268.0\pm96.3$ pA before the biases applied and $261.2\pm97.3$ pA after the biases removed, respectively. With increased biases, the current decreases to $151.7\pm57.7$ and $163.4\pm60.2$ pA under $0.2{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (Figure 3b,f,j), and then $58.0\pm24.7$ and $68.2\pm30.6$ pA under $0.4\mathrm{~V~}$ (Figure  3c,g,k). Under $0.6~\mathrm{V},$ the current becomes negligibly small, measured to be $-1.7\pm2.3$ and $0.5\pm3.6$ pA (Figur $^{3\mathrm{d},\mathrm{h},\mathrm{l}}$ ). It is evident that rather small dif­ ference of photocurrent exists between forward and backward biases, which is clear from the mappings (Figure 3a–h), and can be better appreciated from comparisons of the histogram dis­ tributions (Figure 3i–l). This set of data suggests that at least at the local scale as probed by pc-AFM, the photovoltaic hysteresis seems small despite strong enhancement in polarization under the light illumination.
In order to better quantify the local hysteresis, we carry out principal component analysis[29,31] to decouple variations of photocurrent with respect to space and bias voltage, which con­ verts correlated observations into linearly independent variables called principal components (in space) and modes (in voltage). To this end, the data set $I(x,\gamma,V)$ of photocurrent in terms of spatial coordinate $(x,\gamma)$ and bias voltage $V$ is first reshaped into a 2D matrix form ${\mathbf{I}}(n,\ V)$ , where the original grid array $(x,\ \gamma)$ becomes a 1D vector indexed by $n$ , as schematically shown in Figure S5 (Supporting Information). Singular-value decompo­ sition (SVD) is then carried out, with $\mathbf{I}=\sum_{i=1}^{r}\sigma_{i}u_{i}w_{i}^{\operatorname{T}}$ , where $r$ is the rank of I, left and right singular vectors $\left(\left\{u_{i}\right\}\right.$ and $\{w_{i}^{\mathrm{T}}\})$ reflect spectral and spatial variations of I, respectively, while the singular values $\{\sigma_{i}\}$ indicate their importance. The singular values of PCA are shown in Figure 4a, suggesting that the first mode is dominant and contributes to $75.2\%$ of the information contained in the data. The corresponding mode 1 mapping is shown in Figure 4b, representing the spatial variation of photo­ current regardless of the bias voltage. The first spectral mode as shown in Figure 4c, on the other hand, reflects the variation of photocurrent with respect to forward and backward biases, and indeed very small hysteresis is seen, with the largest differ­ ence in photocurrent at the same bias measured to be $11.1\ \mathrm{pA}$ which is just $6.9\%$ of forward current at that voltage. Mode 2 contributes to $5.8\%$ of the information according to the singular value seen in Figure  4a, and the hysteresis seen in Figure  4d is comparable to mode 1, with the largest photocurrent dif­ ference around $14.4~\mathrm{pA}$ . While this analysis yields valuable information, the local $I V$ curves in Figure  4c,d as measured by pc-AFM is quite different from the macroscopic $J V$ curves measured on a PSC device. This is believed to be contributed by leaking resistance and to a less extent, contact resistance in the probe-sample system.[32,33] Taking into account the leaking resistance as schematically shown in Figure  4e, we recon­ structed local $I V$ curves, which resemble well the macroscopic $J V$ curves measured on a PSC device, as shown in Figure S6 (Supporting Information). Again, rather small hysteresis is observed as seen in Figure 4e, and the hysteresis index, defined as (I × V )BMacxkward − (I × V )FMoarxward , is measured to be just 1.0% at the (I × V )BMacxkward local scale. Note that the open circuit voltage is relatively low due to the lack of hole transport layer.[54,55]
The much-enhanced local polarization by light illumination at the nanoscale, accompanied by only slight ionic migration as well as rather small local hysteresis, suggests that the polariza­ tion is not responsible for the photovoltaic hysteresis even in a polar halide perovskite, and light enhanced polarization is harmless as far as photovoltaic hysteresis is concerned. In order to firmly establish this conclusion, we fabricated a PSC with a configuration of glass/FTO/TiO2/CsFAMA/Spiro-OMeTAD/Ag, as shown by the cross-section SEM image in Figure  5a, to examine its hysteresis. The surface of glass/ $\mathrm{\FTO/TiO}_{2}/$ CsFAMA, as revealed by the top-view SEM image in Figure 5b, shows high-quality dense morphology with no obvious pinholes or cracks, consistent with AFM topography shown earlier. XRD in Figure  5c confirms the phase structure of the triple-cation mixed halide perovskite of CsFAMA, with peaks at approxi­ mately $13.97^{\circ}$ , $19.91^{\circ}$ , $24.46^{\circ}$ , $28.31^{\circ}$ , $31.77^{\circ}$ , $34.96^{\circ}$ , $40.51^{\circ}$ , and $43.16^{\circ}$ corresponding to the diffraction of (101), (012), (021), (202), (211), (122), (024), and (131) planes for CsFAMA.[56] Scanning electron microscopy/energy-dispersive spectroscopy (SEM/EDS) was carried out to map the composition of CsFAMA on glass/ $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ substrate, revealing the dis­ tributions of all the relevant elements (Figure S7, Supporting Information). Macroscopic photovoltaic testing was then car­ ried out, as presented in Figure S8 (Supporting Information), and $J V$ curves measured on the device are shown in Figure 5d under both forward and backward scans with the scan rate of $4\mathrm{~V~}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . They exhibit very small hysteresis, with the corre­ sponding PCEs calculated to be $19.48\%$ and $20.11\%$ , respec­ tively. This results in a low hysteresis index of only $3\%$ , defined as $\frac{\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{backward}}-\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{forward}}}{\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{backward}}}$ ,[57] confirming what we conclude from the local probing at the nanoscale. This hysteresis is slightly larger than what we estimated from local $I V$ curve due to more inter­ faces involved in the device.
It is important to reflect on the generality of our study, par­ ticularly the effect of different compositions. For unary $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , it has been well established that hysteresis is a serious concern, and the primary mechanism responsible is ionic migration,[58] while our previous studies have revealed coexisting polarization and ionic migration in both $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ films and crystals,[25,27] as shown in Figures S9 and S10 (Supporting Information), though it is difficult to resolve their relative contributions to the hysteresis. We also carried out a preliminary study on binary $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})}_{x}\mathrm{(MAPbBr_{3})}_{1-x}$ (FAMA), and found that light illumina­ tion has a negligible effect on both first and second harmonic piezoresponses, as shown in Figure S11 (Supporting Informa­ tion), while the corresponding device also shows rather small hysteresis. For ternary system with a slightly different compo­ sition, there is a recent study reporting that ionic migration occurs under concurrent lightening and electric poling,[59] yet not under either lightening or electric poling alone, suggesting that illumination-induced ionic migration (in the absence of electric poling) is indeed suppressed in ternary perovskites, which is not very sensitive to the ternary composition. All these studies are consistent with the main observation of this work, suggesting that our conclusion is relatively general, and we are planning more systematic investigations spanning a wider com­ position range for ternary perovskites.
In conclusion, we have demonstrated strong photoinduced polarization strain in triple-cation mixed-halide perovskite CsFAMA, while only slight ionic migration is found to be induced by light. As a result, only small photovoltaic hysteresis is observed at both nano- and macroscale, demonstrating that even with the presence of strong polarization, the photovoltaic hysteresis is minimal. The study thus establishes polarization order in triple-cation mixed-halide perovskite under light illu­ mination, and support the concept that the primary mechanism responsible for photovoltaic hysteresis is ionic migration[58] instead of polarization. As such, spontaneous polarization can be taken advantaged to separate photocarriers without compro­ mising the photovoltaic hysteresis of PSCs.
## Experimental Section
Material and Crystalline Structure: Precursor solution of $\mathsf{C s}_{0.05}\mathsf{F A}_{0.81}\mathsf{M A}_{0.74}\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{I}_{2.55}\mathsf{B r}_{0.45}$ (CsFAMA) was prepared in two steps. First, dissolved FAI (1.211  m) and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $(7.275~\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{M})$ in anhydrous DMF:DMSO $(\mathsf{v};\mathsf{v}=4;\mathsf{l}$ ) as (I), MABr (1.425  m) and $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ $(7.500~\mathrm{{s}})$ in anhydrous DMF:DMSO $(\vee:\vee=4:]$ ) as (II), and CsI as a $0.750\mathrm{~m~}$ stock solution in DMSO as (III). Then, mixed and stirred $440~\upmu\up L$ (I), $66~\upmu\up L$ (II), and $44~\upmu\up L$ (III) solutions at room temperature for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ was deposited on glass/FTO substrate as reported elsewhere,[34] and the perovskite precursor solution was spin-coated onto glass/FTO $\slash\Gamma_{1}0_{2}$ substrate under 1000  rpm for $\rceil0\ s$ and 4000  rpm for $60~\mathsf{s}$ . During the second spin-coating step, $68~\upmu\up L$ of anisole was jetted onto the spinning substrate $30\mathrm{~s~}$ prior to the end of the program. The film was then annealed at $\mathsf{l}\mathsf{l}0^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $20~\mathrm{min}$ in a nitrogen filled glove box, and CsFAMA film on glass/FTO $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ substrate was obtained. Cross-sectional and top-view SEM images of glass/FTO/ $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ /CsFAMA were acquired on a Hitachi SU8010 scanning electron microscope, and its X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern was obtained via D8 Advance diffractometer with $\mathsf{C u}$ $\mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation $(40~\mathsf{k V}$ and $40\ \mathsf{m A}$ ) under the scanning rate of $4^{\circ}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ between $10^{\circ}-50^{\circ}$ .
Device and Photovoltaic Properties: Spiro-OMeTAD hole transporting layer was first spin-coated onto glass/FTO/ $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ /CsFAMA under 5000  rpm for $30\mathrm{~s~}$ from a solution with a nominal concentration of $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{101.22\ m g}}$ of Spiro-OMeTAD, $7m L$ chlorobenzene, $40.32~\upmu\up L$ of TBP, $24.5\upmu\up L$ of Li-TFSI stock solution $(520\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ in acetonitrile), and $49\upmu\up L$ FK209 stock solution $(200~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ in acetonitrile). And then $70-80~\mathsf{n m}$ of $\mathsf{A g}$ was thermally evaporated under high vacuum onto Spiro-OMeTAD as top electrode. The photocurrent-density–voltage $(I V)$ characteristics of the solar cells were measured using a Keithley 2400 source under illumination of a simulated sunlight (AM1.5, $\mathsf{l o o\ m w c m^{2}}.$ provided by a solar simulator (Newport 69907) with an AM 1.5 filter, using scanning rate of $4\mathrm{~V~}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ . Light intensity was adjusted with an NREL-calibrated Si solar cell with a KG-2 filter for approximating 1 Sun light intensity. While measuring current and voltage, the cell was covered with a black mask with an aperture close to the active device area. All the measurements of the solar cells were performed under ambient atmosphere at room temperature without encapsulation.
Dynamic Strain Scanning Probe Microscopy for Polarization and Ionic Strain: For ds-SPM measurements, we adopted two different techniques to the region of interest on a commercial AFM system (Asylum Research MFP-3D) in the dark and under illumination. Light illumination was carried out by an unfiltered CREE XLamp MK-R 12  V LED. One is the dual-amplitude resonance tracking, which employs two separate lock-in amplifiers and a digital proportional-integral gain controller (also referred to as feedback loop) to track the resonance frequency.[35,36] In this case, an AC voltage of $2\lor$ was applied to a conductive probe (NanoSensors PPP-EFM) with a spring constant of $2.56\ n\mathsf{N\ n m^{-1}}$ . The two driving frequencies, each sent from one of the amplifiers, are determined through the feedback loop to track the sample-probe resonance frequency $f_{0}$ so that we can get an enhanced sensitivity of response and minimize the cross-talk with topography. Damped simple harmonic oscillator model was then applied to determine the corresponding resonant frequency and quality factor.[46,51] All the measurements were carried out in air ambient over a $2\times2\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}$ area with a scan rate of $2H z$ . The other technique is the custom-developed point-wise first and second harmonic piezoresponse measurement.[25,27,50] For each selected point over the same area as mappings, we performed a series of tunings using an AC voltage varying from 1.2 to $3.0\mathrm{V}$ with an increment of $0.2\mathrm{\:V}$ applied to the probe in turn. Each voltage step contains two tunings, that the probe-sample system is first excited around $f_{0}$ for the first harmonic (linear) response and then around $f_{0}/2$ for the second harmonic (quadratic) response, while responses were always measured around $f_{0}$ .
Photocurrent and Local $1V$ Curves: Photoconductive AFM (pc-AFM) is developed by coupling a conductive AFM (c-AFM, ORCA module on the Asylum Research MFP-3D) with a proper light source. For pc-AFM measurements, we use a standard ORCA cantilever holder with a $500~\mathsf{M}\Omega$ resistor inside and a BudgetSensors Multi75E-G conductive probe with a spring constant of $2.86~\mathsf{n N}~\mathsf{n m}^{-1}$ . The holder possesses a current range of $\pm20$ nA while the typical noise is controlled within 1  pA. To build a close-circuit between the sample and probe, a bias wire mounted on the back of holder is attached to the FTO substrate. We can also apply a DC voltage to the sample when exploring photocurrent–voltage (IV) characteristics. For light source, the unfiltered CREE XLamp MK-R 12 V
LED working at $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{10.2V}}$ is used to illuminate the sample from bottom up in air. Each mapping takes $128\ s$ with the scanning rate of $2\ H z$ where the sequential DC bias voltages are applied one by one without pause. The whole process is realized by MacroBuild, a graphic macro built in AFM software of Asylum Research to automate data acquisition.
Considering possible leaky resistance between the bare perovskite thin film and bottom conductive FTO layer, we added a linear $I V$ curve with a slope of $412\mathsf{p A V}^{-1}$ (or an equivalent resistance of $2.43\ \mathsf{C}\Omega_{\iota}^{\mathfrak{\prime}}$ ) to the first PCA spectral mode when applied bias $<0.6~\mathsf{V}$ to simulate the case that diode was nearly off. When the bias $=0.7$ V, the diode is supposed to be on so that leaking current of the resistance can be ignored. With such a zeroth-order approximation, the first PCA spectral mode of local photocurrent versus forward and backward biases can be reconstructed, which qualitatively agrees with macroscopic measurements. All data-processing mentioned were performed with MATLAB.
